<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamdanid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">890–1004</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Hamdanids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hamdanid dynasty was an Arab Shia family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-taghlib">Banu Taghlib</a> that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the 10th century. One branch held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a> founded the emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 945 and led constant wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. His court attracted the poet al-Mutanabbi and the philosopher al-Farabi. The Aleppo line ended in the early 11th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Hamdanid dynasty" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamdanids_955_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The brothers and their heirs fought on many fronts: against the <b>Hamdanids</b> of Mosul in the north, the Samanids of Khurasan and the Ziyarids of Gurgan in the east, and rival dynasties in Kerman and Oman, which Adud al-Dawla subdued in the late 960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Near al-Mada'in in central Iraq, the armies of the <b>Hamdanids</b> of Mosul and the Baridis of Basra began a four-day battle for Baghdad, the Abbasid capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-16">16 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Byzantine commanders ended a siege begun in November 968 and took the strategically important city of Antioch from the <b>Hamdanid dynasty</b>, recovering it from Arab rule.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The city was governed by appointees of Baghdad and later of the Tulunids, Ikhshidids and <b>Hamdanids</b>, but its garrison and civic leaders acted largely independently.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-tarsus">Emirate of Tarsus</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Hamdanids</b> were a family of the Banu Taghlib who rose to power in Mosul.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-aleppo">Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Hamdanids</b>, from the Arab tribe of Taghlib in the Jazira, rose as Abbasid commanders.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul">Hamdanid emirate of Mosul</a></li></ul></section>
